 the for  people across united kingdom people are  already  hurting enough uh they're really  suffering they're worried about the  increase in heating and energy bills  that's going to come this autumn they're  worried about the higher taxes they're  paying for the conservatives now to say  well it's okay we'll be a trade war i'm  sorry that's just not acceptable  you talk about energy uh costs uh i also  put it to mr gove that before brexit he  did say that that would be cut on energy  bills um that hasn't happened  he's now said that that is because it  would be a five percent cut for  everybody and dukes and princes don't  need that cut  well um uh  the problem with mr gove and the  conservatives is they keep breaking  their promises  this is not the only promise they've  broken they promised not to  raise taxes and they've risen they've  put up taxes they promised not  pensioners that they would keep the  triple lock on pensions they've broken  that promise this is a government that  breaks promises left right and center  and it's hurting people out there but do  you think a cut on the  vat on energy bills is the right way  forward i think a broader cut in vat is  the right way forward i wouldn't just  limit to energy bills food prices are  going up petrol prices are going up the  way to really help people in a very big  and bold way is what liberal democrats  are arguing for which is a two and a  half percent cut in vat down to 17.5  uh that would give the average family  600 pounds  a year so i think that's the sort of  bold measure that's needed you can only  be enough there is it for energy bills  600 quid well it would go some way i  mean we have a another set of policies  linked to that which would help the most  vulnerable because 600 pounds would be  for everybody uh for the average family  but there are pensioners uh disabled  people people on very low incomes who  will need some extra help so we've  argued that what's called a warm home  discount which takes money directly off  people's energy bills that that should  be doubled and the number of people who  could get that would be should be  increased so that's real help to people  who are struggling where are you on  secure stammer and um beer gate  well um at the weekend uh i was  suggesting that uh he would have to  consider a position if he got a fixed  penalty notice he would he would have to  um and then  earlier this week he actually said  that's what he positioned he would take  that if he got a fixed penalty notice  from the durham police he would resign  um and i was actually  quite pleased to see him say that  because i think that shows  integrity and willingness to to accept  the consequences and i just wish that  the prime minister showed some integrity  yeah but in march he did say i think it  was in march and the leader of the  opposition said because the prime  minister was being investigated by the  police he should resign and now he's  being investigated by the police but  he's not resigning  well uh listen he's got to make his own  decision right right right well he i  i've said that i think it's at least  good that he said he's prepared to  resign if he gets a fixed penalty notice  uh i just know the prime minister's had  a fixed penalty notice he's not resigned  i also note there's a possibility that  the prime minister might get even more  fixed penalty notices so you know the  prime minister's in charge of our  country he's supposed to be helping  people uh who are struggling he's not  doing that and my worry is he's he's not  giving leadership he's now become a very  weak leader because he's dogged by this  these crisis we're almost out of time  but my question is um  should the leader of the opposition who  is now uh part of a criminal  investigation resigned given that that's  what he said the prime minister should  do well um uh i i haven't said that i've  been very clear on my position uh  throughout this he  he he's got to decide he's his own uh  position i actually called on the prime  minister to resign not when there's a  police investigation i called for him to  resign before that and i explained why  he had said he he he he lied to  parliament about parties and it turned  out with allegra stratton's uh comments  that there were parties so my uh problem  with the prime minister is not just that  he broke the law but he's dishonest okay  it's good to see you sired thank you for  joining us on the programme this morning  thank you

